    category: "finance",
    faq: [{ question: "How is CAGR calculated?", answer: "CAGR equals (end÷start)^(1÷years)−1, with multiple as end÷start and gain as end−start. For example, $10,000 growing to $20,000 in 5 years gives about 14.87% CAGR, a 2x multiple, and a $10,000 gain. It smooths yearly volatility into one steady rate for comparison. See /terms." }, { question: "Is this CAGR calculator financial advice?", answer: "No, this CAGR figure is for informational growth analysis only, not investment advice. Dividends, fees, taxes, inflation and sequence risk are excluded from the 14.87% illustration on $10,000 to $20,000. Consult a qualified professional about personal choices and see /terms before investing." }, { question: "Is this CAGR calculator free and private?", answer: "Yes. Growth CAGR math stays in-tab only. Start end sums run here with no transfer and no account. For instance, $10,000 to $20,000 stays local, needs no net, and erases on shut. It is study math only, not advice. See /terms." }, { question: "Why can 21% three-year CAGR trail 14% five-year wealth?", answer: "Because duration magnifies compounding base, $10,000 at 21.6% for 3 years reaches $18,000 ($8,000 gain), while 14.87% for 5 years reaches $20,000 ($10,000 gain). Short sprints show hotter rates but smaller harvests. Annualize only alongside absolute multiples. Model both horizons here carefully. See /terms." }, { question: "How does lumpy cashflow distort CAGR versus XIRR?", answer: "CAGR assumes single start-to-end path, so $10,000 plus mid-course $5,000 top-up skews rate versus XIRR timing each flow. SIP tranches at varying NAVs demand XIRR near 13.2% versus naive 14.87% CAGR. Use CAGR for lump sums, XIRR for instalments. Contrast methods via /sip-calculator. Method guidance only, not investment counsel. See /terms." }],
