    guide: [{ heading: "What CAGR means", body: "CAGR distills volatile multi-year journeys into one constant yearly compounding pace linking start to finish. $10,000 reaching $20,000 in 5 years implies 14.87% CAGR with 2x multiple and $10,000 gain, while $18,000 in 3 years implies hotter 21.6% yet smaller $8,000 gain. Growth leaderboard table aids benchmarking: | Path | Multiple | CAGR | $10k-$20k 5yr | 2x | 14.87% | $10k-$18k 3yr | 1.8x | 21.6% | $10k-$15k 5yr | 1.5x | 8.45% |. Longer doubles can beat briefer spikes in dollars. Funds, equities, SIPs, and revenues rely on CAGR for horizon-neutral contrasts. Our CAGR calculator outputs rate, multiple, and gain privately offline. Edge case: zero or negative start values fracture exponentiation, requiring absolute narratives. Limitation: smooths drawdowns, hiding sequence trauma. Project forward via /compound-interest-calculator. Analytical lens only, not investment advice. See /terms." }, { heading: "How CAGR, multiple, and gain are calculated", body: "CAGR equals end divided by start raised to one divided by years minus one, multiple equals end divided by start, gain equals end minus start. For example, $20,000 over $10,000 gives 2x and $10,000 gain, power one-fifth minus one gives 14.87% yearly. A second illustration: $18,000 over $10,000 over 3 years gives 1.8x, $8,000 gain, 21.6% pace. Horizon distortion table warns comparisons: | End $10k start | Years | CAGR | Gain | $15,000 | 5 | 8.45% | $5,000 | $18,000 | 3 | 21.6% | $8,000 | $20,000 | 5 | 14.87% | $10,000 |. Hotter rate need not mean richer harvest. Edge case: interim $5,000 withdrawals invalidate single-path assumption, demanding XIRR. Limitation: excludes dividends reinvestment timing, fees, levies, and inflation dilution. Dissect instalment reality via /sip-calculator. Formula exposition only, never portfolio counsel. See /terms." }, { heading: "CAGR worked example: $10k to $20k in 5 years is 14.87 percent", body: "Enter $10,000 start, $20,000 end, 5 years to see 14.87% CAGR, 2x multiple, $10,000 gain, then try $18,000 end to watch rate dip to 12.47% and multiple to 1.8x. What-if grid sharpens intuition: | Start-end 5yr | CAGR | Multiple | $10k-$20k | 14.87% | 2x | $10k-$18k | 12.47% | 1.8x | $10k-$15k | 8.45% | 1.5x |. Two thousand less end shaves 2.4 points. Edge case: business revenue from $0 launch to $50,000 breaks division, use absolute runway instead. Limitation: backward-looking smoothing cannot predict forward volatility, regime shifts, or survivorship bias. Pair retirement compounding via /retirement-calculator. Informational growth analysis only, not professional investment guidance before allocating capital judiciously. Copy figures for board decks after verifying audited statements thoroughly. See /terms." }]
