    faq: [{ question: "How is my loan EMI calculated?", answer: "EMI = P×r×(1+r)^n÷((1+r)^n−1), where P is principal, r is monthly rate annual÷12÷100 and n is months. Example: Rs 10L at 9% for 20 years gives r=0.0075, n=240 and EMI near Rs 8,997 with interest near Rs 11.59L. Zero rate uses P÷n." }, { question: "Is this EMI calculator financial advice?", answer: "No. It is for informational planning only, never financial advice. Actual EMI varies with processing fees, insurance, floating rates, and prepayment terms. For example, a 1% fee on Rs 10 lakh adds Rs 10,000 upfront. Consult your lender about sanction terms and see /terms before signing." }, { question: "Does my loan data stay on this device?", answer: "Yes. EMI inputs never leave this device. Principal, rate, and months run in-tab offline with no upload and no sign-up. For example, a Rs 50,00,000 loan test stays local, works net-off, and wipes on shut. See /terms for details." }, { question: "How accurate is this EMI estimate?", answer: "It stays close for fixed-rate amortizing loans, typically within rounding of official lender schedules. Processing fees, taxes, insurance, and floating-rate resets are excluded from the math. For example, a variable 9% moving to 9.5% shifts EMI notably. Verify against your sanction letter and see /terms." }, { question: "Which Indian lender rates should I compare?", answer: "Contrast SBI near 8.50%, HDFC Bank near 9.00% and Bank of Baroda near 9.15% as illustrative starting points. For example, Rs 10 lakh over 20 years moves from roughly Rs 8,678 to Rs 9,089 monthly across that band. Weigh processing levies, reset clauses and CIBIL-linked spreads before choosing. See /terms." }, { question: "Why does prepayment in year 3 save so much?", answer: "Early lump sums attack principal while interest compounding is steepest. For example, Rs 5 lakh prepaid in year 3 on Rs 10 lakh at 9% slashes tenure by about 11 years and saves near Rs 9.1 lakh interest. Later prepayments help less because principal already shrank. Confirm lock-in and foreclosure terms first. See /terms." }],
    howTo: [{ name: "Enter principal", text: "Type principal Rs 10,00,000, subtract down payment Rs 1,00,000 if any, confirm net loan Rs 9,00,000, currency and schedule start month before continuing." }, { name: "Set rate & tenure", text: "Enter annual interest 9% fixed, tenure 20 years totalling 240 months, select monthly rests, then verify rate type and reset clause carefully." }, { name: "View EMI", text: "Review EMI near Rs 8,997, total interest near Rs 11.59 lakh, total payable Rs 21.59 lakh, yearly amortization chart, then export CSV summary." }, { name: "Compare scenarios", text: "Compare 8.5% versus 9.5% rates and 15 versus 20 year tenures, test Rs 5 lakh prepayment in year 3, observe savings shift instantly." }],
    guide: [{ heading: "What an EMI is", body: "An Equated Monthly Installment (EMI) is the fixed monthly payment repaying principal plus interest over tenure, with early instalments interest-heavy and later ones principal-heavy. For example, Rs 10,00,000 at 9% for 20 years (r=0.0075, n=240) gives EMI near Rs 8,997, total payable near Rs 21.59 lakh and interest near Rs 11.59 lakh. Indicative bank pricing varies by CIBIL, occupation and benchmark: | Lender | Illustrative fixed rate | EMI on Rs 10L/20y | SBI | 8.50% | Rs 8,678 | HDFC Bank | 9.00% | Rs 8,997 | Bank of Baroda | 9.15% | Rs 9,089 |. Spreads, MCLR, repo linkage and processing fees shift actual offers. Edge case: zero-interest loans fall back to principal divided by months. Limitation: fixed-rate amortisation only, excluding floating resets and charges. Compare variants with /loan-calculator. This is educational planning only, not financial advice. See /terms." }, { heading: "How EMI is calculated", body: "Lenders use EMI = P×r×(1+r)^n/((1+r)^n-1), where P is principal, r is monthly rate (annual÷12÷100) and n is months. For Rs 10 lakh at 9% for 20 years, r=0.0075 and n=240, yielding Rs 8,997; total equals Rs 8,997×240 (Rs 21.59 lakh) minus principal leaves Rs 11.59 lakh interest. Prepayment transforms cost: a Rs 5 lakh lump sum in year 3 (after 36 instalments) cuts balance from about Rs 9.35 lakh to Rs 4.35 lakh, shortening tenure by roughly 11 years and saving near Rs 9.1 lakh interest. Banks gate eligibility via FOIR (total EMIs near 50% of net monthly income) and CIBIL (750+ earns finer spreads). Edge case: part-payments during lock-in attract foreclosure levies. Limitation: ignores insurance, taxes and variable resets. Check borrowing room with /home-loan-eligibility-india. Informational only, not advice. See /terms." }, { heading: "EMI worked example: Rs10L at 9 percent for 20 years", body: "Take Rs 10 lakh at 9% for 20 years: pay Rs 8,997 monthly across 240 instalments, totalling Rs 21.59 lakh with Rs 11.59 lakh interest. Shorten to 15 years (n=180) and EMI rises to about Rs 10,143, yet interest falls to near Rs 8.26 lakh, saving over Rs 3.3 lakh. Alternatively keep 20 years but prepay Rs 5 lakh in year 3; interest drops by near Rs 9.1 lakh and the loan closes years early, beating a simple tenure cut for cash-rich borrowers. Test FOIR impact: Rs 60,000 salary allows about Rs 30,000 EMIs, so existing Rs 8,000 obligations leave Rs 22,000 housing room. Edge case: floating-rate hikes can erase prepayment gains if spreads reset upward. Limitation: excludes processing levies, stamp duty and penalties. Validate sanction specifics with your lender and model extra payments via /mortgage-overpayment-calculator. Educational estimate only, not financial advice. See /terms." }]
