    category: "finance",
    faq: [{ question: "Which 2026 tax tables does this paycheck calculator use?", answer: "It uses 2026 federal brackets with the standard deduction, plus FICA payroll tax with the $184,500 Social Security base and Medicare. State tax is a simplified estimate, such as a 5% flat placeholder you can override. For example, an $85,000 single salary shows federal, FICA, and state splits. See /terms." }, { question: "Is this paycheck calculator tax advice?", answer: "No. It is a planning estimate only, never tax advice. Local tax, credits, pre-tax benefits, and W-4 adjustments are excluded from the math. For example, 401k contributions change withholding notably. Verify against your pay stub or payroll team and see /terms." }, { question: "Does my paycheck data stay private in this tool?", answer: "Yes. Paycheck withholding math stays fully on your phone. Salary frequency sums run in-tab with no transfer and no sign-in. For example, $85,000 stays local, needs no net, and wipes on close instantly. Planning guidance only, not tax advice for payroll decisions. See /terms." }, { question: "Why does $40 hourly sometimes net less than $85,000 salary?", answer: "Because hourly $40 times 2,080 equals $83,200 base, yet overtime premiums, unpaid holidays, and benefits gaps alter totals versus salaried $85,000 with PTO. Hourly FICA identical at 7.65%, but withholding cadence differs weekly. Model 40 versus 45 hours here, inspect PTO valuation. See /terms." }, { question: "How do 401k and HSA elections cascade through withholding?", answer: "Pre-tax $4,000 401k plus $2,000 HSA reduce federal taxable $85,000 to $79,000, trimming federal $1,320 at 22% marginal while FICA still hits $85,000 except HSA via cafeteria. State follows federal after adjustments. Input $0 versus $6,000 deferrals here. Benefits illustration only, not tax counsel. See /terms." }],
